examples.question_text:
Which vector is equal to $\underset{\sim}{a}$, and which is its negative? Explain why the remaining vector is neither.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[draw=gridgrey,xstep=7mm,ystep=7mm] (0,0) grid (8,6);
\draw[->,thick,black] (0,0) -- (4,2);
\path (2,1) node[above left] {$\underset{\sim}{a}$};
\draw[->,thick,black] (4,3) -- (8,5);
\path (6,4) node[above left] {$\underset{\sim}{b}$};
\draw[->,thick,black] (4,6) -- (0,4);
\path (2,5) node[above left] {$\underset{\sim}{c}$};
\draw[->,thick,black] (5,1) -- (7,2);
\path (6,1.5) node[below right] {$\underset{\sim}{d}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Sketch $-\underset{\sim}{p}$ on the grid.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[draw=gridgrey,xstep=7mm,ystep=7mm] (0,0) grid (7,6);
\draw[->,thick,black] (1,5) -- (5,2);
\path (3,3.5) node[above right] {$\underset{\sim}{p}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
$\underset{\sim}{p}$ goes $4$ squares right and $3$ down. Reverse both steps: $-\underset{\sim}{p}$ goes $4$ left and $3$ up, with the same length.

One possible sketch is shown. Its tail can be placed anywhere the arrow fits.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\definecolor{answerblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[draw=gridgrey,xstep=7mm,ystep=7mm] (0,0) grid (7,6);
\draw[->,thick,black] (1,5) -- (5,2);
\path (3,3.5) node[above right] {$\underset{\sim}{p}$};
\draw[->,thick,draw=answerblue] (6,1) -- (2,4);
\path (4,2.5) node[below left] {$-\underset{\sim}{p}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Reversing the original arrow also represents the negative: $\overrightarrow{BA}=-\overrightarrow{AB}$.

From $A$ to $B$, the horizontal step is $2-(-3)=5$ and the vertical step is $4-1=3$.

From $C$ to $D$, the horizontal step is $6-1=5$ and the vertical step is $1-(-2)=3$.

Both arrows go $5$ units right and $3$ up, so they have the same magnitude and direction. Hence $\overrightarrow{AB}=\overrightarrow{CD}$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=5mm,y=5mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[draw=gridgrey,xstep=5mm,ystep=5mm] (-4,-3) grid (7,5);
\draw[->,thick,black] (-3,1) -- (2,4);
\draw[->,thick,black] (1,-2) -- (6,1);
\path (-3,1) node[below left] {$A$};
\path (2,4) node[above right] {$B$};
\path (1,-2) node[below left] {$C$};
\path (6,1) node[above right] {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

From $A$ to $B$, the step is $1-(-2)=3$ units right and $3-1=2$ units up.

An equal vector repeats this step from $C$:

$\begin{align*}D&=(2+3,\,-1+2)\\&=(5,1).\end{align*}$

The negative reverses the step, giving $3$ units left and $2$ down from $E$:

$\begin{align*}F&=(4-3,\,4-2)\\&=(1,2).\end{align*}$

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\definecolor{answerblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{answerred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[draw=gridgrey,xstep=7mm,ystep=7mm] (-3,-2) grid (6,5);
\draw[->,thick,black] (-2,1) -- (1,3);
\draw[->,thick,draw=answerblue] (2,-1) -- (5,1);
\draw[->,thick,draw=answerred] (4,4) -- (1,2);
\path (-2,1) node[below left] {$A$};
\path (1,3) node[above left] {$B$};
\path (2,-1) node[below left] {$C$};
\path (5,1) node[above right] {$D(5,1)$};
\path (4,4) node[above right] {$E$};
\path (1,2) node[below left] {$F(1,2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
